\begin{abstract}
\color{SoftWhite}
This thesis presents a cosmological analysis of open‑universe curvature fields, hyperbolic geodesic divergence, and large‑scale structure evolution under non‑zero spatial curvature. The work derives the hyperbolic coordinate transformation used to map Euclidean particle matrices onto curved FLRW manifolds and evaluates the physical consequences for cosmic expansion, angular magnification, and structure formation. Numerical stability and autonomous computational frameworks are discussed in the context of large‑scale cosmological simulations.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Open‑universe cosmologies ($\Omega_k > 0$) require mathematical frameworks capable of describing geodesic behavior in hyperbolic space. Observational evidence from high‑redshift galaxy surveys suggests that even small curvature deviations can produce measurable effects across large comoving distances. This thesis derives the relevant transformations and analyzes their physical implications.

\section{FLRW Metric and Curvature}
The FLRW metric for an open universe is:

\begin{equation}
ds^2 = -c^2 dt^2
      + a(t)^2 \left[
          \frac{dr^2}{1 - \Omega_k r^2}
          + r^2 (d\theta^2 + \sin^2\theta\, d\phi^2)
        \right].
\end{equation}

\subsection{Hyperbolic Spatial Geometry}
Spatial slices correspond to hyperbolic manifolds. Distances expand faster than linearly, and geodesics diverge more rapidly than in Euclidean space. This divergence produces characteristic angular magnification effects.

\section{Hyperbolic Coordinate Transformation}
The curvature‑dependent scaling factor is:

\begin{equation}
S(r) = \frac{\sinh(r\sqrt{\Omega_k})}{r\sqrt{\Omega_k}}.
\end{equation}

Coordinates transform as:

\begin{equation}
\vec{x}_{\text{warped}} = S(r)\,\vec{x}_{\text{raw}}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Velocity Field Adjustment}
Velocity fields scale inversely:

\begin{equation}
\vec{v}_{\text{warped}} = \frac{\vec{v}_{\text{raw}}}{S(r)}.
\end{equation}

This preserves geodesic consistency.

\section{Small‑Radius Behavior}
Direct evaluation of $S(r)$ produces a $0/0$ singularity at $r \to 0$. Using L'Hôpital's rule:

\begin{equation}
\lim_{r \to 0} S(r) = 1.
\end{equation}

A stable Taylor expansion is:

\begin{equation}
S(r) = 1 + \frac{1}{6}\Omega_k r^2 + \mathcal{O}(r^4).
\end{equation}

